## Developer-Facing Output

```xiom
pub interface Debug {
  fn fmt(self: &Self, f: &mut Formatter) -> Result[(), FmtError];
}
```

### Implementing Debug

```xiom
#[derive(Debug)]  // Auto-implement for simple types
pub type Point = { x: Float64; y: Float64; }

// Manual implementation:
impl Debug for Point {
  fn fmt(self: &Point, f: &mut Formatter) -> Result[(), FmtError] {
    return f.debug_struct("Point")
      .field("x", &self.x)
      .field("y", &self.y)
      .finish();
  }
}

fn main() {
  let p = Point{ x: 10.5, y: 20.3 };
  io.dprintln(p);  // Point { x: 10.5, y: 20.3 }
}
```

### Debug vs Display

```xiom
let money = Money{ cents: 1099 };
io.println(money);    // $10.99  (Display — user-friendly)
io.dprintln(money);   // Money { cents: 1099 }  (Debug — raw state)
```

### Debug in Error Messages

```xiom
fn process(data: &Vec[Int]) -> Result[(), Str] {
  if data.len() == 0 {
    return Err("Empty data: ${data:?}");  // Uses Debug format
  }
  return Ok(());
}
```

`:?` in format strings triggers Debug output. `{}` triggers Display.

### Derive is Best

For most types, `#[derive(Debug)]` is all you need. Manual Debug is for types that need custom debug representations (e.g., hiding sensitive fields).

